{"step_index":1076,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:36:42Z","thinking":"**Deducing graph radius bound**\n\nIf the graph's radius is less than or equal to the total domination number, and the total domination number is less than or equal to the size of *any* total dominating set, then the radius must always be less than or equal to the size of that set. This derives from the definition of eccentricity and the concept of a ball within the graph. Then there must exist a vertex whose eccentricity is less than or equal to the set's size, implying that the ball around that vertex within that radius is the entire graph. However, the precise Lean proof needs further refinement.\n\n\n**Exploring total domination and radius**\n\nInvestigating a proof relating the total domination number and radius by leveraging properties of a total dominating set and distances between vertices. Each vertex has a neighbor in the total dominating set. Considering connected components within the dominating set, and examining the shortest path from a central vertex. A shortest path from the center vertex to a farthest vertex is considered to connect the vertices.\n\n\n**Analyzing connected components' domination**\n\nSingle vertices in the dominating set can cover at most two edges on the path. Adjacent vertices in the set span at most three. A connected component of size *m* can dominate a subpath of length *m* + 1 due to the graph's structure. Components along the path are connected, therefore there are no uncovered edges between components on $P$.\n\n\n\n**Examining the relationship between diameter and radius**\n\nDiameter, which can exceed the size of the total dominating set, is explored through examples to clarify the relationship to radius. The example path P_6 has a diameter greater than |S|. This implies radius must be less than or equal to the total domination number. This is because the center vertex ensures the maximum distance (radius) is bounded.\n\n**Relating the domination number and path length**\n\nInvestigating the total domination number on a path of length 2R+1 where each adjacent vertex pair covers 4 path vertices. Using this relation it will be possible to determine a lower bound of the size of *S*. The minimum size of S is at least 2 times the total number of pairs that could be needed.\n\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"find / -name \\\"SimpleGraph\\\" 2>/dev/null\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Finding SimpleGraph in Mathlib\"","toolSummary":"\"Find SimpleGraph in Mathlib\""}}]}
